<h2>Why the law starts from a presumption</h2>
<p>The presumption of legitimacy exists to protect children from the collateral damage of their parents' disputes. A maintenance case is about subsistence; the law refuses to let it become a paternity trial by default. Section 116 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam makes birth during a valid marriage <em>conclusive</em> proof of legitimacy — a category of proof that evidence cannot contradict — subject to a single statutory exception: proof that the spouses had no access to each other at any time when the child could have been begotten. "Access" means the existence of opportunity for marital relations, not proof that relations occurred.</p>

<h2>The framework in maintenance proceedings</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Stage</th><th>What the court asks</th></tr>
<tr><td>Pleading</td><td>Has the respondent specifically pleaded non-access for the relevant period — separation, imprisonment, residence abroad — or merely denied paternity?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Foundation</td><td>Is there prima facie material of non-access? Travel records, separate residence, judicial orders. Without it, the Section 116 presumption applies and the test is refused.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Necessity</td><td>Is a DNA test eminently necessary, or can the issue be resolved on the existing evidence? The test is a last resort, not a shortcut.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Child's interest</td><td>What does the test do to the child's legitimacy, privacy and social standing? The child is not a party to the matrimonial conflict, and courts weigh this heavily.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Interim maintenance cannot wait for science</h2>
<p>A paternity dispute does not suspend the child's need to eat. Courts routinely grant interim maintenance under Section 144 BNSS on the strength of the presumption, leaving the respondent's challenge for trial. A respondent who believes he has a genuine non-access case should still comply with interim orders — default invites enforcement and costs, and compliance is without prejudice to the defence.</p>

<h2>Practical notes for both sides</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>For respondents: the defence is built on documents proving absence of opportunity — postings, passports, decrees of separation. Plead them with dates in the first reply; a foundation laid late looks like an afterthought.</p>
<p>For mothers: resist roving DNA demands by invoking the conclusiveness of Section 116 and the child-welfare line of reasoning; where a test is nonetheless directed and is scientifically sound, consider that a favourable result ends the dispute decisively.</p>
<p>For both: remember that legitimacy and maintenance are distinct — even a child whose legitimacy is disputed may claim maintenance as an illegitimate child under Section 144 BNSS, which covers legitimate and illegitimate minor children alike. The paternity fight, in other words, rarely extinguishes the liability; it only changes its doctrinal route.</p>
</div>
